            "type": "assistant_message",
            "status": "completed",
            "title": null,
            "text": "Security review round 2 for the login rate limiter change on branch fix/login-limiter-per-account.\n\nScope: src/auth/limiter.ts, src/auth/limiter.test.ts, the Redis key layout and the lockout response.\n\nChecks performed:\n- Key construction: the account key uses the normalised email (lower-cased, trimmed, Unicode NFKC); the IP key uses the first untrusted hop of X-Forwarded-For, and only behind the configured proxy list. Both parts are hashed before they enter the Redis key, so no key injection is possible.\n- Counter windows: both counters use a sliding 15-minute window with a TTL refresh on each failure. A successful login resets only the account-and-IP counter, so a victim who logs in does not clear an attacker's distributed counter.\n- Lockout semantics: a lock needs 5 failures for the account from one IP, or 20 failures for the account across all IPs. A single attacker IP can no longer lock an arbitrary account with five attempts.\n- Response: locked requests receive HTTP 429 with a Retry-After header, and the body does not reveal whether the account exists.\n- Timing: the limiter check runs before password hashing, so locked requests consume no hashing time; unlocked requests always hash, unknown accounts included.\n- Logging: lockouts are logged with the account hash and the IP prefix, never with the plain email address or the password.\n- Configuration: both thresholds and the window length are read from auth.rateLimit in the service configuration, with the documented defaults applied when the block is absent.\n- Tests: limiter.test.ts covers the shared-IP case, the distributed case and the reset after success, and every case asserts both counters.\n\nFinding status:\n- S1 (account lockout as denial of service): resolved by the dual-counter rule above; the account-only lock is gone.\n\nNew findings: none.\n\nReview outcome: approved with no open findings.",
            "updatedAt": "2026-10-21T10:00:00.000Z"
